Edition No. 32 — Monday, 2 November 2026

10 stories: Fourteen disappearances and 71 arrests: Human Rights Watch calls for an end to the post-election crackdown, as State House answers Ian Khama and the treason bail ruling nears (lead); 'I don't have a sense of entitlement': Mweetwa says no MP is owed a ministry, denies planning a new party with other dropped ministers, and stays with the UPND; Printed before the withdrawals: the 5 November ballot papers land from Dubai with the Mandevu names still on them, six wards prepare to vote, and Lubinda questions Article 52(6); 'An undisclosed location': Emmanuel Banda's mother asks the High Court to make the police produce her son, as a UPND official says his 2024 escape should count against bail; Drones 'sent to Zambia in error': the Economic and Financial Crimes Court at Chongwe convicts GMX Systems Zambia and two men over under-declared imports, with K2.1 million to pay; 'Manifestly inadequate': the prosecution asks the Court of Appeal to lengthen Malanji's four years and Yamba's three and to overturn the hotel acquittal, Kalemba reports; The 'wandering zone': the Bank of Zambia says a security thread that sits in a slightly different place does not make a banknote counterfeit, and lists the features to check; Thirty-four bales and the bill for burning them: the standards agency seizes used underwear worth K37,350 from a Lusaka shop and restates the 2006 ban on second-hand undergarments; Three from three and a 40-point margin: Munali Suns crush Botswana's Dolphins 96–56 at NASDEC, Douglas Kandulu scores 35, and Harare Kings are next on Monday afternoon; Five draws from six and a goalless Kitwe derby: Zanaco are the only winners of Super League week six, while Fashion Sakala scores and makes two for Al-Fayha.


